---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Compressor from "../components/Compressor.astro";
import FAQ from "../components/FAQ.astro";

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "Should I even include a photo on a US job application?",
    a: "In the US, UK, Canada, and Australia, resume photos are generally discouraged and can work against you due to anti-discrimination norms in hiring. This tool is mainly useful when a specific portal explicitly asks for a headshot upload as a separate field — government job portals, some ATS systems, and creative-industry applications (acting, modeling, on-camera roles) being the most common cases — or when applying to a role or country where a photo is standard practice.",
  },
  {
    q: "What size and format do hiring portals usually expect for a photo?",
    a: "It varies by platform, but keeping a headshot under roughly 100–200 KB in JPG format is a safe target for most systems. Some applicant tracking systems (ATS) reject unusual formats like HEIC or TIFF outright, so JPG is the safest default unless the portal says otherwise.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is this the same as compressing my resume file?",
    a: "No — this tool compresses image files (JPG, PNG, WebP), not PDF or Word documents. If your resume itself is too large to upload, you'll need a PDF compressor instead; this tool is specifically for a separate photo/headshot upload field.",
  },
  {
    q: "What's a good target size if the portal doesn't specify one?",
    a: "100 KB is a reasonable default for a headshot-style photo — small enough to clear most upload limits, large enough to still look sharp at typical display sizes. If the portal gives an explicit number, use that instead.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Resize a Photo for Job Application Uploads (Exact KB) | KB Compressor"
  description="Compress a headshot or photo to the exact KB size a job application or hiring portal requires. Free, no upload — runs in your browser."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-10 pt-14 text-center sm:pt-20">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white sm:text-4xl">
      Compress a photo for job application portals
    </h1>
    <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      Some hiring and government job portals ask for a headshot photo under a strict KB
      limit, separate from your resume file. Set the limit from the portal and download a
      photo sized to match.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-16">
    <Compressor
      defaultTargetKB={100}
      presets={[50, 100, 150, 200]}
      contextNote="This tool compresses photos, not PDF resumes. If it's your resume file that's too large, you'll need a PDF compressor instead."
      ratioWarningKB={null}
    />
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-6 px-4 pb-20 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Getting a clean headshot under the limit</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Portals that request a headshot photo alongside your application usually cap it well
        below what a phone camera produces by default — a photo straight off a modern
        smartphone can run several megabytes, while the upload field might cap out at
        100&nbsp;KB or less. Compressing to a specific KB target, rather than dragging a vague
        quality slider and hoping, means you can hit the limit on the first try instead of
        guessing and re-uploading.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">When a photo actually belongs on a job application</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Practice varies a lot by country and industry. In the US, UK, Canada, and Australia,
        attaching a photo to a resume is generally discouraged — many employers and hiring
        systems actively avoid looking at applicant photos to reduce bias risk, and some ATS
        platforms strip images out entirely. Where this tool is genuinely useful is the
        narrower case of a portal with a dedicated photo upload field — government job
        applications, some corporate ATS systems, and creative fields like acting, modeling,
        or on-camera roles, where a headshot is part of the actual application requirements
        rather than an optional add-on to a resume.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">A resume photo is not a resume file</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        It's worth being clear about what this tool does and doesn't do: it compresses image
        files only. If a job portal is rejecting your resume itself for being too large,
        that's almost always a PDF or Word document size problem, not an image compression
        problem, and needs a different tool. This page is specifically for the separate photo
        or headshot upload field some portals include alongside the resume upload.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Formats that avoid upload problems</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        JPEG is the most universally accepted format across hiring portals and produces the
        smallest file at acceptable quality, which is why it's the default output here.
        Formats like HEIC (the default on many iPhones) or TIFF are rejected outright by a
        lot of applicant tracking systems — if your photo is in one of those formats, convert
        it to JPG or PNG first (most phones offer this as a share or export option), then use
        this tool to hit your target size.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQ items={faqItems} />
</Layout>
